Then consider universal supports

275
00:23:11.141 --> 00:23:16.003
available to many students. Instructions
can be offered in spoken, written and

276
00:23:16.004 --> 00:23:20.675
visual forms. Students may have flexible
ways to demonstrate learning, with

277
00:23:20.676 --> 00:23:25.159
movement breaks or sensory tools available
when useful. Targeted support

278
00:23:25.160 --> 00:23:30.084
responds to a more specific need. For
example, a small group might benefit from

279
00:23:30.085 --> 00:23:35.072
key vocabulary being introduced before a
lesson, or from additional organisation

280
00:23:35.073 --> 00:23:39.429
support during independent work.
Individual adjustments respond to the

281
00:23:39.430 --> 00:23:43.597
particular student’s access and
participation needs. Examples might

282
00:23:43.598 --> 00:23:48.333
include a quieter workspace for an
assessment, additional processing time, a

283
00:23:48.334 --> 00:23:53.005
personalised task breakdown or another way
to demonstrate learning. What is

284
00:23:53.006 --> 00:23:57.994
appropriate depends on the student and the
context, informed by consultation and

285
00:23:57.995 --> 00:24:03.109
evidence. Look back at the design check.
What is available to everyone? What might

286
00:24:03.110 --> 00:24:07.024
need to be targeted? What needs individual
adjustment? Academic

287
00:24:07.025 --> 00:24:11.570
adjustments sit within this wider picture.
A diagnosis alone doesn’t tell

288
00:24:11.571 --> 00:24:16.495
us which adjustment will help. Connect the
response with your observations, the

289
00:24:16.496 --> 00:24:21.042
